<Base title="AI, Data & Editorial Policy — The V Spot Network" description="How The V Spot Network uses AI, protects information and keeps people responsible for the work." type="WebPage">
  <main class="wrap page policy">
    <header>
      <p class="kicker">The working rules behind the studio</p>
      <h1>AI, data & editorial policy</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">AI can help with the research, the rough cut and the first draft. It does not get the final say. People do.</p>
      <p class="edition">Published 30 October 2026 · Owner: Vinny O’Brien · Review every six months</p>
    </header>

    <p>This policy explains how The V Spot Network uses AI tools and handles information in its media work. The Network is produced by Vinny and Co Consulting Limited. Client consulting work is also governed by the relevant client contract, data processing terms and engagement instructions; those may impose stricter requirements.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="scope">
      <h2 id="scope">What this covers</h2>
      <p>It applies to The V Spot’s shows, essays, newsletters, production and related work, and to people working on that work. It covers AI tools, transcription and editing services, connected apps and other service providers that may handle information.</p>
      <p>For consulting engagements, we verify the specific account plan, data terms, connector permissions and client authorisation before using a tool with client information. Until those checks are recorded for an engagement, client-confidential and personal data must not be entered into AI tools or connected apps for that work. We do not use client names, figures, findings, documents or recordings in V Spot content without written permission.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="principles">
      <h2 id="principles">How we use AI</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>People remain accountable.</strong> AI may assist with research, transcription, editing, analysis and drafting. A person reviews material before publication or before it is used to advise a client.</li>
        <li><strong>We check the work.</strong> AI output can be wrong, incomplete, biased or confidently made up. Important factual claims are checked against reliable evidence, and uncertainty is not dressed up as certainty.</li>
        <li><strong>AI does not make consequential decisions about people.</strong> We do not use it to decide who gets a job, credit, education or access to essential services.</li>
        <li><strong>We keep client work separate.</strong> Client information is used only for the agreed engagement, in the agreed environment, and only to the extent necessary. It does not migrate into media work, shared examples or another client’s project.</li>
        <li><strong>We respect the people in the room.</strong> We tell meeting participants when recording or transcription is being used, explain the purpose, and follow the applicable contract and privacy requirements.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="data">
      <h2 id="data">Information we put into AI tools</h2>
      <p>We do not put passwords, authentication tokens, payment-card or bank details, government identifiers, or special-category personal data into general-purpose AI chats.</p>
      <p>Client-confidential or personal information may be processed only where the client’s instructions and contract allow it, the tool is approved for that engagement, and the amount shared is limited to what the task needs. When a second model is used for review, personal identifiers are removed or replaced wherever practicable.</p>
      <p>Information from one client is not used in another client’s work. Demonstrations and reusable examples use invented data, not client records.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="tools">
      <h2 id="tools">Tools, access and service providers</h2>
      <p>We use a limited set of business and production tools for collaboration, storage, scheduling, transcription and AI-assisted work. The tools used for a particular client engagement depend on the client’s instructions, contract and the task. We will provide the relevant service-provider and processing information to clients on request.</p>
      <p>Connected apps can expose information or allow actions in other services. We check their access before using them with client data and do not treat default permissions as proof of least privilege. Client-system writes, sends and deletions must be individually authorised and reviewed by a person; AI recommendations alone never authorise a change. Until connector permissions are verified for an engagement, connected apps must not be used to access or change that client’s records.</p>
      <p>Our required account baseline is unique credentials, multi-factor authentication where available, and access review as engagements and tools change. A tool or device is not approved for client work until the applicable security settings have been checked. Data locations and terms vary by provider and plan, so those are confirmed for the tools used on each engagement.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="content">
      <h2 id="content">AI in published content</h2>
      <p>AI may assist with research, captions, editing, graphics, audio or video. Editorial responsibility stays with The V Spot. We do not present a synthetic person’s words, voice or likeness as a real person’s statement. We identify AI-generated or materially manipulated content when required by law or when a reasonable audience could otherwise be misled.</p>
      <p>We also follow the rules that apply to copyright, privacy, recording and the rights of guests and contributors. Sponsors do not control our editorial conclusions.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="rights">
      <h2 id="rights">Privacy, incidents and questions</h2>
      <p>We handle personal information under applicable data protection law and our privacy information. Where we process information for a client, we follow that client’s documented instructions and notify them of a relevant personal-data incident without undue delay, in line with the contract and applicable law.</p>
      <p>To ask about this policy, request information about a tool used for a client engagement, or raise a privacy concern, email <a href="mailto:vinny@thevspotnews.com">vinny@thevspotnews.com</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <aside aria-labelledby="governance">
      <h2 id="governance">Review and status</h2>
      <p>We review this policy every six months and when our tools, work or legal obligations materially change. This page describes our working standards; publishing it is not a certification, audit report or guarantee that every operational control has been independently verified. Client contracts and applicable law take precedence where they set a stricter requirement.</p>
      <p>Relevant frameworks include the EU and UK GDPR and the EU AI Act. The EU AI Act’s AI literacy duty applies to providers and deployers, and its Article 50 transparency requirements apply from 2 August 2026, subject to the provisions and transition dates in the Regulation.</p>
      <p class="sources">Official references: <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj">Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/ai-literacy-questions-answers">EU Commission: AI literacy</a> · <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/transparency-obligations-under-article-50-ai-act">EU Commission: Article 50 transparency</a> · <a href="https://www.dataprotection.ie/en/organisations/know-your-obligations/controller-and-processor-relationships">Irish DPC: controller and processor relationships</a></p>
    </aside>
  </main>
</Base>
